## Why

The reviewer's first screen on the public URL is the feed, and it is the one screen that needs no tap and no code. Today it shows an idea as text, two percentage pills and a yellow button labelled **Copy**; the chart the brief asks for (SHOULD 6, "so the trade is obvious") only appears after tapping that button, whose label promises a trade rather than a chart. A reviewer who browses without tapping never sees the idea visualised — which is exactly the observation we received ("I think you missed *visualise a signal on the chart*"). The brief's own bar is "a smart 12-year-old could use it"; a card a child can read at a glance is the fix, and it is small.

**Tier served:** SHOULD 6 (visualise the signal) and the product rule "genuinely simple UI".

## What Changes

- **Every card draws the idea on its coin's own price.** A mini chart under the headline shows the coin's last hour as a line, with the idea drawn over it — dashed lines for Entry (yellow), Take profit (green) and Stop loss (red) — and a **yellow dot at the live price**, plus one plain sentence ("now $79,855 · on its way to the take profit"). Each coin gets its own chart, as in social-trading feeds (fomo.family-style). When the coin's candles cannot be read the card falls back to a strip (Stop loss on the left, Entry between, Take profit on the right, the same dot and sentence) drawn from the idea alone, so a card never depends on a fetch to render.
- **The feed carries live prices and the last hour of candles.** `GET /api/signals` and the server-rendered feed include, for every market that has a live idea, its live mid and its last 60 one-minute candles (a handful of markets, two calls each, failures tolerated per market). The route and the page share one loader instead of duplicating the read.
- **The card's direction is a picture, not a pill.** The headline becomes "BTC goes up ↑" in green (or "goes down ↓" in red) so the direction is readable before any word.
- **The card button says where it goes.** "See it on the chart" instead of "Copy"; the one-tap copy stays on the detail, where the brief puts it. Expired cards keep "See how it went".
- **Copies close in time share one chart marker** ("Ben, Cid copied" or "3 copied") instead of overlapping labels — a defect seen on the deployed chart, where two copies nine minutes apart still collided on a 200-bar chart.
- README and the status table say where the visualisation lives (feed strip + detail chart).

## Capabilities

### New Capabilities

None.

### Modified Capabilities

- `copy-trade-signals`: the feed-card requirement gains the per-coin mini chart (strip as fallback), the live "now" marker, the direction headline and the new button label; the listing requirement gains live prices and candles in the feed response; the detail-chart requirement groups copies close in time into one marker.

## Non-goals

- No interactive chart in the feed (no zoom, no crosshair, no chart library per card): the mini chart is a static SVG line; the interactive chart stays on the detail.
- No change to authoring, copying, the order path or the passcode.
- No new colours; two small SVG components (chart and strip).

## Impact

- `lib/signals/math.ts` (pure strip geometry + marker clustering, tested), `lib/signals/feed.ts` (new shared loader), `lib/decibel/markets.ts` (`getPrices` and `getCandlesFor` for several markets), `lib/schemas.ts` (`SignalList.prices`, `SignalList.candles`).
- `components/SignalCard.tsx`, new `components/IdeaChart.tsx` and `components/IdeaStrip.tsx`, `components/SignalDetail.tsx` (marker clustering), `app/page.tsx` and `app/api/signals/route.ts` (use the loader).
- No new dependencies. The feed poll makes one price call and one candles call per distinct live market every 10 s.
